    <string-array name="facts">
        <item>Harappan traders used polished stone cube weights in a regular series (1, 2, 4, 8, 16…) about 4,500 years ago.</item>
        <item>The metre was first defined in 1793 as one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator.</item>
        <item>The ivory scale found at Lothal, Gujarat, is marked in divisions of about 1.7 mm, one of the finest rulers of the ancient world.</item>
        <item>Since 2019 the kilogram has been defined by the Planck constant, not by a metal cylinder kept near Paris.</item>
        <item>Jaipur\'s Samrat Yantra is a sundial 27 m tall. Its shadow moves about 1 mm every second.</item>
        <item>A nautical mile, 1,852 m, is about one minute of latitude, which is why ships and planes use it.</item>
        <item>A tola is 11.66 g, and it is still used in gold and silver markets across South Asia.</item>
        <item>Anders Celsius first set 0 for boiling water and 100 for freezing; the scale was flipped after his death.</item>
        <item>A lakh is 1,00,000 and a crore is 1,00,00,000. The words come from Sanskrit laksha and koti.</item>
        <item>Strictly, 1,024 bytes is a kibibyte (KiB); a kilobyte (kB) is 1,000 bytes. Many apps, this one included, use 1,024.</item>
        <item>A bigha is not one size: about 1,338 m² in Bengal, about 2,529 m² across much of North India.</item>
        <item>The United States is one of only a few countries that has not adopted the metric system for everyday use.</item>
        <item>Ancient Indian timekeeping counted the day in 60 ghadis of 24 minutes each.</item>
        <item>India passed its metric law in 1956 and began switching over on 1 October 1958.</item>
    </string-array>
